1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,120
And now, for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:12,200
Here's a little rule of thumb for you as a Scoutmaster.

3
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:19,480
If you think it goes without saying, it almost certainly does not.

4
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:24,920
If you think it goes without saying, it almost certainly does not.

5
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:27,840
Yeah, like, here's what I mean.

6
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:29,880
Bring your rain gear.

7
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:32,720
That doesn't go without saying.

8
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:37,519
If you put that in the fire, it will explode.

9
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:39,280
Do not put it in the fire.

10
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:41,660
That does not go without saying.

11
00:00:42,060 --> 00:00:42,880
Needs to be said.

12
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:46,019
If you do that, you will hurt yourself.

13
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:47,500
Do not do that.

14
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:52,019
Sometimes, really needs to be said, right?

15
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:54,840
Okay.

16
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:59,420
So if you think it goes without saying, it probably doesn't.

17
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:02,800
This is podcast number 46.

18
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:07,060
Hey!

19
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:33,600
Well, welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast. This is Clark Green.

20
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:42,600
Hey, I really appreciate those of you who've been in touch over the past week. Will, who is an Assistant Scoutmaster in Sharples, Florida, wrote this. He says,

21
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:58,740
Great show, Clark. I'm a big fan of your jokes, but two accountant jokes in the last podcast might be pushing it. Yeah. Well, I got to tell you, Will, they weren't actually accountant jokes because that would be that would just be beyond the pale.

22
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:05,919
Well, they were economist jokes, you know, not to put too fine a point on it, but yeah.

23
00:02:06,919 --> 00:02:16,200
Keep up the great work. I continue to enjoy and learn from you and your email contributors. Also, I am listening to all the archive shows. Thanks for making those available.

24
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:35,120
If you want to see the podcast archive, it's available through scoutmaster.typepad.com. That's the home of the blog and the podcast. And if you look in the upper left-hand corner, you will see a link to the archive of Scoutmaster Podcast.

25
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:46,540
Last time we talked about boards of review, and one of my thoughts was that these should not be a retest of scout skills. Larry Geiger sent in this comment. Larry keeps me honest. Thanks, Larry.

26
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:54,820
It's an absolutely essential thing that scouts be tested on specific scout skills in their scoutmaster conferences and boards of review.

27
00:02:55,440 --> 00:03:04,299
The adult leaders in the troop cannot know whether the scout leaders are performing their leadership duties and preparing their scouts if they don't ask specific questions.

28
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:10,019
Can you tie a bow in? What's the difference between hypo and hypothermia? That, you know, that type of thing.

29
00:03:10,399 --> 00:03:15,560
How are the scout leaders doing teaching skills? I mean, are the scouts learning? And there's really only one way to know.

30
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:26,920
And I agree with you, Larry. Specific questions, those are good. But they can be done in the context of asking questions without being a retest of things.

31
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:34,700
Larry goes on to say, if we're going to take scouts backpacking and canoeing and climbing, we had better be working to prepare them for those experiences.

32
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:39,000
We and the boy leaders are responsible to ensure that they have the requisite skills.

33
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:46,360
In a lot of ways, this is a simple issue of quality control. The scoutmaster conference and board of review are learning situations.

34
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:51,840
In my experience, most scouts have great answers to almost every skill question that gets asked.

35
00:03:51,840 --> 00:04:02,220
I realize this is somewhat of a fine line for some people because some adults feel called to be the ones that administer the scouting SAT at boards of review.

36
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:06,300
And I realize we need to prevent that. Well, Larry, those are excellent thoughts.

37
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:13,160
And what we really need to do, I think, is define the difference between review and retest.

38
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:19,100
And we also need to consider exactly what would constitute reasons for a scout failing a board of review.

39
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:26,920
And Larry went on to say, we do get hung up on the review and test words sometimes.

40
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:34,700
The only scoutmaster conferences I can remember where I have had to ask a scout to wait a week or two is when they don't know the oath and law.

41
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:39,440
In every case, I remember they have come back and have memorized it exactly.

42
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:44,719
I fully agree that not passing a board of review should be very rare.

43
00:04:45,140 --> 00:04:49,780
Well, I think there's still some talking to do about this and some things to define.

44
00:04:49,900 --> 00:04:56,580
And I'd be interested in knowing what you have to say on the subject and maybe questions or concerns or comments.

45
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:01,840
And we'll have the email address that you can send those to at the end of the podcast here.

46
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:04,640
Bob from Murfreesboro, Tennessee got in touch.

47
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:07,960
He says, I've been actively following your podcast over the last several months.

48
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:09,840
And let me tell you, I've thoroughly enjoyed it.

49
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:12,200
I've been a scoutmaster for 13 years.

50
00:05:12,780 --> 00:05:16,080
And your podcast has been both inspirational and entertaining.

51
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:21,659
I greatly appreciate you taking the time to put together your podcast and look forward to each new chapter.

52
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:22,440
Well, thank you, Bob.

53
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:24,340
I really appreciate your listening.

54
00:05:25,039 --> 00:05:29,539
Jeff from Georgia chimed in and he said, I'm entering my second year as a scoutmaster.

55
00:05:29,919 --> 00:05:31,340
We're planning a court of honor.

56
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:34,400
I have read your blog and listened to your podcasts on this.

57
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:37,880
I was just wondering if you have an outline of how your scouts approach this.

58
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:42,719
I really have nothing specific to go by other than just some general statements.

59
00:05:43,359 --> 00:05:47,820
I know that there is no one set way, but I would like to see just some kind of way.

60
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:56,940
And Jeff, I went ahead and sent you a copy of our court of honor script that we've used for ages and ages.

61
00:05:56,940 --> 00:06:04,760
And if you're interested in looking at that, drop me an email and we'll make sure that you get a copy.

62
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:10,960
This week on the blog, it's holiday season and there are a couple of posts about gifts for your favorite scoutmaster.

63
00:06:10,960 --> 00:06:16,460
What I did is I put a post up about books and most of the books I've referred to on the podcast.

64
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:28,359
And if you haven't taken the opportunity to get those yet, there's some links on the blog of the book reviews and the podcasts they're associated with and the way that how you can get them.

65
00:06:29,159 --> 00:06:34,060
And I also put up a link with five or six pieces of essential scoutmaster gear.

66
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:43,560
So if you're doing shopping or somebody's shopping for you, you know, go to the blog and take a look and maybe that'll help you with your holiday shopping.

67
00:06:44,140 --> 00:06:48,380
You can find that at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

68
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:57,400
If you listen to the podcast using iTunes, iTunes has a deal where you can leave a review or rate the quality of the podcast.

69
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,680
And some of you have been doing that in the past couple of weeks and I really do appreciate it.

70
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:10,840
And I wanted to make sure to say thank you to Phil Trost and to Tony and to Adam for leaving some very nice compliments.

71
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:12,880
I really do appreciate it.

72
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:18,700
In this podcast and Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less, we're going to talk about the scouting process.

73
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:29,440
And then I've got a little Scout Masters minute for a piece that I found that describes scouting to people who may not be familiar with the program.

74
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:33,360
It's a unique way of doing it and it might be helpful to you.

75
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:35,360
So that's plenty to do.

76
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:36,320
So let's get started.

77
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:40,600
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

78
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:50,100
Well, we know what the mission is, right?

79
00:07:50,100 --> 00:08:01,600
The mission of the Boy Scouts of America, as they stated, is to prepare young people to make ethical and moral choices over their lifetimes by instilling in them the values of the Scout Oath and law.

80
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:04,600
That's right out of the BSA mission statement.

81
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:22,520
Now, if our goal were to produce a product, you know, a universal model citizen of some sort, a universal leader, it would make sense to have production efficiencies and kind of extensive quality control programs.

82
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:25,200
But that's not our mission, is it?

83
00:08:25,940 --> 00:08:27,359
We don't produce a product.

84
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:36,059
Prepare young people to make ethical and moral choices over their lifetimes by instilling in them the values of the Scout Oath and law.

85
00:08:36,460 --> 00:08:38,059
You know, what we do is we play a game.

86
00:08:39,179 --> 00:08:49,939
And the gist of the game is to offer a set of circumstances that challenge Scouts to think for themselves, to make decisions for themselves, and to develop some skills.

87
00:08:49,940 --> 00:08:54,380
This process prepares them for a lifetime of challenges.

88
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:58,320
When they leave us prepared, we've really completed the mission.

89
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:03,200
And there's really no way to test and measure those results.

90
00:09:03,960 --> 00:09:09,520
Now, I go back to this subject often because I think it's one of the tougher things to get about Scouting.

91
00:09:09,520 --> 00:09:23,060
Most of us are involved in a business of production or the science of management, and we're used to having some kind of demonstrable, you know, benchmarks and results that we can look at and say that we've done our jobs.

92
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:32,240
When we become Scout Masters, we are eager to make good and often apply the principles of our professional work to our Scouting job.

93
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:37,060
And that might mean that, you know, well, we try and do it as efficiently as possible.

94
00:09:37,420 --> 00:09:42,540
We try and look at the question of quality control and optimum production practices.

95
00:09:43,860 --> 00:09:49,660
And I think it can be frustrating because Scouts don't behave like a product on a production line.

96
00:09:50,900 --> 00:10:00,440
What we need to do is, I think, liberate ourselves from the idea of producing anything to being the ones who maintain a process.

97
00:10:00,440 --> 00:10:05,700
We need to learn and maintain this game really well.

98
00:10:06,140 --> 00:10:08,340
We need to protect the integrity of the game.

99
00:10:08,660 --> 00:10:17,820
We need to keep it open and vibrant so that any boy that comes to us has a set of challenges that will take him through a process that will achieve the final mission.

100
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:22,000
We don't look for measurable results.

101
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,700
Oh, there's all kinds of numbers to look at.

102
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:30,800
There's all kinds of things that we can crunch and see if we're staying on track.

103
00:10:30,900 --> 00:10:36,380
But those aren't as important as making sure that the process works.

104
00:10:37,460 --> 00:10:41,640
And that is where this wonderful intangible growth results.

105
00:10:41,960 --> 00:10:43,760
And we achieve the mission of Scouting.

106
00:10:43,760 --> 00:11:13,740
We'll be right back.

107
00:11:13,760 --> 00:11:38,120
That's right.

108
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:41,300
It's time for a Scoutmaster's Minute.

109
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:51,400
Here's something I ran across.

110
00:11:51,520 --> 00:11:56,440
I had the occasion where I needed to explain Scouting to people who it was not a Scouting audience.

111
00:11:57,040 --> 00:11:58,800
And I was kind of casting around a little bit.

112
00:11:58,860 --> 00:11:59,800
I found this online.

113
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:00,880
This isn't my own work.

114
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:04,220
It was a piece that is in a national publication.

115
00:12:05,140 --> 00:12:14,380
And I thought it would be interesting to share it with you because I found it a particularly effective and interesting way of sharing Scouting with people.

116
00:12:14,380 --> 00:12:17,140
And what it begins with is the Scout shirt.

117
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:21,480
If we look at a Scout shirt, we know a few things.

118
00:12:21,580 --> 00:12:24,900
First, it's your traditional Boy Scout item.

119
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:29,120
I mean, Boy Scouts have been wearing a Scout uniform for 100 years.

120
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:34,000
Second, each Scout shirt is a little bit different.

121
00:12:34,100 --> 00:12:35,640
Now, there's a standard starting place.

122
00:12:35,740 --> 00:12:38,420
There's this blank shirt that you can buy at a Scout shop.

123
00:12:38,900 --> 00:12:41,860
But then each Boy's shirt starts to look different.

124
00:12:42,280 --> 00:12:45,240
There are different sizes and different patches.

125
00:12:45,940 --> 00:12:48,040
All Scout shirts have the same basic shape.

126
00:12:48,180 --> 00:12:51,340
But beyond that, there is a broad variety.

127
00:12:51,340 --> 00:12:55,680
Scout shirts are generally decorated on just one side, the outside.

128
00:12:55,940 --> 00:12:57,540
And that's the part that you show off.

129
00:12:58,420 --> 00:13:02,380
And the Boy Scouting program itself is a lot like a Scout shirt.

130
00:13:02,940 --> 00:13:04,840
First, it's a traditional program.

131
00:13:05,720 --> 00:13:11,900
Scouts today enjoy many of the same activities that they did when Baden-Powell first created the program a century ago.

132
00:13:12,860 --> 00:13:16,900
And there's a lot of other things that haven't changed either much over time.

133
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:20,080
Like the Scout Oath and the Scout Law.

134
00:13:20,080 --> 00:13:25,660
The Scouting program has three aims or purposes that shape it.

135
00:13:26,560 --> 00:13:31,000
And those are character development, citizenship training, and physical and mental fitness.

136
00:13:31,640 --> 00:13:35,440
Boy Scouts and Scouting adults also each have unique experiences.

137
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:37,800
Everybody's time in Scouting is different.

138
00:13:38,760 --> 00:13:43,600
And just like patches, Boy Scouts and Scouting adults have a variety of colors and shapes

139
00:13:43,600 --> 00:13:48,460
and personalities and skills and even political persuasions.

140
00:13:48,460 --> 00:13:49,560
We're not all red.

141
00:13:49,660 --> 00:13:50,340
We're not all blue.

142
00:13:50,780 --> 00:13:53,760
There's lots of different colors out there.

143
00:13:54,140 --> 00:13:57,320
But we're all Boy Scouts because we strive towards the same goals.

144
00:13:57,820 --> 00:14:02,680
And we agree the Scout Oath and Law is a good basis for character development

145
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:06,900
and citizenship training and developing physical and mental fitness.

146
00:14:08,420 --> 00:14:13,860
Boy Scouting is a lot like a Scout shirt because it has a showy side and a plain side.

147
00:14:13,860 --> 00:14:17,480
The showy side of the program is what attracts boys.

148
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:20,440
The fun, the outdoor adventure, the things that they do.

149
00:14:20,840 --> 00:14:22,840
That's the colorful stuff.

150
00:14:22,920 --> 00:14:26,640
The stuff you look at and point to and say, that's cool and I want to do that.

151
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:34,940
But as parents and adults, we more tend to think of the inside rather than the showy stuff on the outside.

152
00:14:34,940 --> 00:14:39,740
We look at what the Scout shirt or the Scout program is made of.

153
00:14:39,880 --> 00:14:41,860
We want to know what kind of fabric it is.

154
00:14:41,940 --> 00:14:44,440
We want to know how it fits and how it wears.

155
00:14:45,640 --> 00:14:49,080
We're glad that our boys are drawn to the colorful stuff on the outside.

156
00:14:49,540 --> 00:14:52,460
But what really matters to us is on the inside.

157
00:14:52,460 --> 00:15:04,900
Well, thanks for listening.

158
00:15:05,660 --> 00:15:07,140
Really do appreciate it.

159
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:12,800
You can read the Scoutmaster blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

160
00:15:13,460 --> 00:15:20,640
You can follow us at Scoutmaster blog on Facebook and ScoutmasterCG on Twitter.

161
00:15:22,460 --> 00:15:31,900
You can subscribe to the Scoutmaster podcast on iTunes.

162
00:15:32,060 --> 00:15:35,660
And when you do, feel free to leave a comment or review or rating.

163
00:15:36,460 --> 00:15:45,360
And you can always email me, Clark Green, with your comments and questions at ClarkGreen at gmail.com.

164
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:53,780
That's C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N at gmail.com.

165
00:15:53,780 --> 00:16:16,120
The Scoutmaster blog and the Scoutmaster podcast are not official publications of the Boy Scouts of America.

166
00:16:16,120 --> 00:16:19,540
Nor are they endorsed or sponsored by the Boy Scouts of America.

167
00:16:19,680 --> 00:16:25,460
No, it's just me talking into a microphone, trying to lend a hand to Scout leaders and have a bit of fun along the way.

168
00:16:26,960 --> 00:16:31,260
Now, I know that goes without saying, but, you know, we say it anyway.

169
00:16:31,260 --> 00:16:46,900
Well, if you listen this long, you get a bonus, bonus joke.

170
00:16:47,420 --> 00:16:52,060
There's a duck and he walks into the pharmacy and he looks at the pharmacist and says,

171
00:16:52,600 --> 00:16:55,040
Hey, give me a tube of chapstick and put it on my bill.

172
00:16:55,040 --> 00:17:10,200
Well, before we go, let's hear from our founder, Sir Robert Baden-Powell.

173
00:17:11,399 --> 00:17:12,020
Sir Robert?

174
00:17:12,960 --> 00:17:15,280
Good luck to you and good camping.

175
00:17:16,440 --> 00:17:17,480
Why, thank you, Sir Robert.

176
00:17:18,819 --> 00:17:19,899
Until next time.

177
00:17:25,040 --> 00:17:26,139
Die act.

178
00:17:55,040 --> 00:18:25,020
Thank you.

179
00:18:25,040 --> 00:18:55,020
Thank you.

